WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.570
Roronoa Zoro is one of One Piece’s greatest
characters.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.980
Hell, he’s one of manga’s greatest characters.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.010
But why?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.080
Maybe because he has the most unique and badass
sword fighting style in all of anime, or because

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.830
of that time he earned Mihawks respect with
his sense of honor, or the fact that he was

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:24.840
prepared to sacrifice that honor, his dream
and even his life for Luffy.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.840
But the love for Zoro goes a lot deeper than
just his bad ass feats.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.230
I mean you gotta love his personality

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:37.540
[clip of Zoro with shit personality]

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:43.090
All jokes aside, I’ve talked about why Zoro
is such an awesome character a lot on this

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:49.370
channel, but really you can boil it down to
just 3 things, that make him one of manga’s

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:50.370
greatest character.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.370
BACKSTORY

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.370
(Backstory until he joins Luffy)

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.500
As you know, Zoro started out as a strong-willed
kid already determined to become the strongest

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.059
swordsman in the world, even before ever meeting
Kuina and their promise to each other.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:04.879
We know that Zoro is somehow related to the
Shimotsuki clan from Wano, but ironically

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:11.930
grew up in Shimotsuki village, a colony of
former Wano citizens in the East Blue.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.000
He trained at the Villages Dojo, where he
was able to beat anyone, but the dojo master’s

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.720
daughter Kuina.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.240
After their promise to each other that one
of them would become the strongest swordsman

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.260
in the world, Kuina tragically died under
mysterious circumstances.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:32.790
Her father says she fell down the stairs,
but it is strongly implied that she might

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:37.540
have taken her own life, because Kuina felt
that women were physically inferior to men

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.170
and could never make up for that difference.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:48.610
This cemented Zoro’s dream even more and
he spent his entire youth training.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.500
He became a bounty hunter to pay for his bills.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.159
One day, in a village called shell town, he
saved a little girl from the marine captain’s

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:00.829
son helmepo.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:07.070
In exchange for not being labeld a criminal
by the marines, Zoro agreed to be tied to

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.599
a pole in front of the marine’s headquarter
and not eat for a month.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:14.300
A deal that the marines never intended to
uphold.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:20.819
So, despite being a lone wolf, Zoro decided
to accept Luffy’s offer to join his crew.

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:26.060
And everything after that is history with
countless epic fights and moments for the

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.569
future strongest swordsman in the world.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:29.569
PART I

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:34.820
But the love for Zoro goes a lot deeper than
just bad ass feats.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:40.030
It’s the character’s personality that
makes him resonate with so many fans and make

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.680
him one of the most popular characters in
all of One Piece, sometimes even outranking

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.489
Luffy himself in that regard.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:56.029
What are the attributes and values that best
define the Straw Hats’ Moss-Headed swordsman?

00:02:56.029 --> 00:02:58.591
First and foremost is strength.

00:02:58.591 --> 00:03:03.280
There’s no shortage of fans who speculate
that Zoro might be even stronger than Luffy

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.890
(though it helps that blades are one of Luffy’s
weaknesses).

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:13.220
Zoro is driven to become the world’s greatest
swordsman - a feat that will take immeasurable

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.220
strength.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.110
Second is discipline.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:20.599
There is no easy path or shortcut to the type
of strength that Zoro seeks.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:26.850
While the rest of the crew “makes merry”
on the ship, Zoro is constantly working out,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.400
lifting the biggest weights known to man.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.510
He knows that he can only achieve his dream
through diligent training.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.220
Third is honor.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.689
Despite his ferocious power, he is morally
upright.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:39.670
He will always make what he perceives to be
the honorable choice.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:45.840
For instance, he refused to let Mihawk strike
his back and instead takes his defeat standing

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.010
upright instead of turning tail and running
away.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.130
The final value that defines Zoro is altruism.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:58.700
Even though, he has a singularly focused pursuit
of his goal, he is willing to lay down his

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.690
life for others.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:05.230
Even his own dream, to become the world’s
greatest swordsman, is not his alone.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:09.120
He fights for this dream just as much to honor
Kuina’s memory.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.629
Our very first introduction to Zoro exemplified
these core values.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:17.949
When Koby first told Luffy about the rumors,
we were introduced to a terrifying monster

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:21.380
- a bloodthirsty, superhuman pirate hunter.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.830
But when we saw him in the stocks, he showed
great discipline and honor.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:30.550
He went without food and water for days on
end and he did it for the sake of an innocent

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:31.550
family.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:35.900
He did the honorable thing in allowing himself
to be imprisoned in exchange for the family’s

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:36.900
lives.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.180
And when he asks Luffy to feed him the rice
balls that Helmeppo smashed on the ground,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.930
it shows another honorable layer to his personality.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:49.560
The fact that he was so strongly defined as
a character right from the beginning shows

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.910
how steadfast Zoro truly is in his convictions.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:53.910
BRIDGE

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:59.070
To look even deeper into Zoro’s character,
though, we have to look beyond the values

00:05:03.296 --> 00:05:04.865
What motifs and themes does Zoro portray?

00:05:04.865 --> 00:05:09.006
And what real-world connections does Oda-Sensei
use in developing his character?

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:10.006
PART II

00:05:10.006 --> 00:05:11.006
Themes

00:05:11.006 --> 00:05:14.705
Religion among swordsmen

00:05:14.705 --> 00:05:19.526
Many significant swordsmen in One Piece are
actually connected with one religion or another.

00:05:19.526 --> 00:05:22.296
For instance, Shanks is connected with Nordic
mythology.

00:05:22.296 --> 00:05:26.765
He’s the captain of a viking ship and his
one arm suggests parallels to the one-armed

00:05:26.765 --> 00:05:28.145
Norse deity Tyr.

00:05:28.145 --> 00:05:31.776
Dracule Mihawk, the current strongest swordsman
in the world, is connected with symbolism

00:05:31.776 --> 00:05:37.305
from Christianity - or at least Gothic vampire
literature - which itself is linked directly

00:05:37.305 --> 00:05:40.956
with Christianity.

00:05:40.956 --> 00:05:45.765
His black blade, Yoru, makes his silhouette
appear as a giant Christian cross behind him.

00:05:45.765 --> 00:05:48.936
Zoro, however, appears to have connections
to Buddhism.

00:05:48.936 --> 00:05:53.026
This is mostly evident in his attack names,
such as his move “Crossing the Six Paths”

00:05:53.026 --> 00:05:57.145
and Asura, a type of demigod within Buddhist
and Hindu religions.

00:05:57.145 --> 00:06:02.835
Both of these religions in turn, were influenced
by Zoroastrianism, from which Zoro might even

00:06:02.835 --> 00:06:05.675
derive his name.

00:06:05.675 --> 00:06:08.055
Swords as a sign of respect

00:06:08.055 --> 00:06:12.365
The swordsmen in One Piece seem to follow
a code of honor.

00:06:12.365 --> 00:06:15.966
Swords themselves can even be seen as a sign
of respect.

00:06:15.966 --> 00:06:21.466
When Zoro first encounters Mihawk, he jumps
at the chance to fulfill his goal.

00:06:21.466 --> 00:06:26.666
If he defeats the Warlord, then he’ll become
the world’s greatest swordsman.

00:06:26.666 --> 00:06:32.406
Mihawk, surely no stranger to such challenges,
thinks that Zoro is just another kid who thinks

00:06:32.406 --> 00:06:35.985
he has what it takes to survive in the Grand
Line.

00:06:35.985 --> 00:06:41.196
He doesn’t respect Zoro and therefore doesn’t
even use a sword against him.

00:06:41.196 --> 00:06:43.426
He uses a tiny knife.

00:06:43.426 --> 00:06:48.596
However, once Zoro demonstrates how honorable
he truly is by declaring that scars on the

00:06:48.596 --> 00:06:55.016
back or a swordsman’s shame, Mihawk recognizes
Zoro as a worthy rival with genuine potential.

00:06:55.016 --> 00:06:59.205
Out of respect, he uses Yoru to deal the finishing
blow of the duel.

00:06:59.205 --> 00:07:03.816
During Zoro’s fight with King, we see what
a dishonorable swordsman looks like.

00:07:03.816 --> 00:07:09.096
King doesn’t follow a similar moral code
to Zoro - instead he uses tricks such as his

00:07:09.096 --> 00:07:13.205
hooked sword to try and disarm his opponent
rather than fight a genuine duel.

00:07:13.205 --> 00:07:19.056
Also, any honorable swordsman would hit subscirbe
at this point of the video.

00:07:19.056 --> 00:07:20.056
Bushido

00:07:20.056 --> 00:07:22.406
It should come as no surprise that Zoro is
influenced by Samurai.

00:07:22.406 --> 00:07:24.795
We’ve learned that he has heritage in Wano.

00:07:24.795 --> 00:07:29.276
Shimotsuki Village in East Blue, where Zoro
was raised, was founded by samurai from Wano

00:07:29.276 --> 00:07:31.316
who fled after Kaidou’s takeover.

00:07:31.316 --> 00:07:35.026
Not only that, but Princess Vivi literally
calls Zoro “Mr. Bushido.”

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:39.865
For those who don’t know, “Bushido”
is another name for the samurai code or “way

00:07:39.865 --> 00:07:41.096
of the samurai.”

00:07:41.096 --> 00:07:45.615
Throughout the various periods of Japanese
history, Bushido has evolved with the time.

00:07:45.615 --> 00:07:51.015
However, one popular summation of its tenets
comes from Nitobe Inazo of the Meiji Period.

00:07:51.015 --> 00:07:59.956
The eight virtues he extolled were: righteousness,
courage, compassion, respect, honesty, honor,

00:07:59.956 --> 00:08:01.176
loyalty, and self-control.

00:08:01.176 --> 00:08:07.116
Obviously, these are all virtues that Roronoa
Zoro embodies.

00:08:07.116 --> 00:08:12.065
Going by Bushido, he’s even more “samurai”
than the samurai of Wano.

00:08:12.065 --> 00:08:13.065
BRIDGE

00:08:13.065 --> 00:08:16.826
Now we’ll look at Zoro even more closely
- more than looking at him as an individual,

00:08:16.826 --> 00:08:18.836
how does he fit into the story of One Piece?

00:08:18.836 --> 00:08:19.945
What’s his role in the crew and what is
his overall story arc?

00:08:19.945 --> 00:08:42.596
How does Zoro change throughout the story
and what does that teach us about his character?

00:08:42.596 --> 00:09:20.406
PART III

00:09:20.406 --> 00:09:23.745
Zoro serves two main roles in the Straw Hat
crew.

00:09:23.745 --> 00:09:29.496
While technically not named as the first mate,
he clearly fits the part.

00:09:29.496 --> 00:09:36.995
He’s Luffy’s right hand and has been there
since the beginning of Luffy’s adventure.

00:09:36.995 --> 00:09:40.195
As the first mate, he’s not just a subordinate
to Luffy, though.

00:09:40.195 --> 00:09:43.316
Instead he acts as Luffy’s counter balance.

00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:49.056
He steps in as a leader when Luffy is incapacitated
and even holds the crew together in its times

00:09:49.056 --> 00:09:50.496
of greatest turmoil.

00:09:50.496 --> 00:09:56.136
He’s made such a name for himself as a noteworthy
pirate that, like Killer, he’s considered

00:09:56.136 --> 00:10:00.356
part of the Supernovas despite not being a
captain.

00:10:00.356 --> 00:10:04.966
Zoro’s official role on the crew is that
of the swordsman.

00:10:04.966 --> 00:10:09.676
He’s one of the deadliest and most powerful
members of the crew, likely second only to

00:10:09.676 --> 00:10:10.676
Luffy.

00:10:10.676 --> 00:10:17.315
When there’s a battle to fight, Zoro is
the first to volunteer to cut down the opponent.

00:10:17.315 --> 00:10:19.736
It’s no coincidence, either, that Zoro uses
blades.

00:10:19.736 --> 00:10:22.286
One of Luffy’s biggest weaknesses is cutting
attacks, since he can resist blunt attacks

00:10:22.286 --> 00:10:24.216
and even gunfire.

00:10:24.216 --> 00:10:32.896
However, Zoro can go sword-to-sword with anyone
who could threaten to cut down his captain.

00:10:32.896 --> 00:10:39.236
Zoro’s character arc is the story of an
individual overcoming a single central flaw.

00:10:39.236 --> 00:10:43.576
And we’re not talk about his being directionally
challenged: his other fatal flaw.

00:10:43.576 --> 00:10:45.896
Roronoa Zoro is a lone wolf.

00:10:45.896 --> 00:10:51.016
In his quest to become the world’s greatest
swordsman, he’s all but given up friends

00:10:51.016 --> 00:10:55.875
and camaraderie in exchange for an all-encompassing
focus on his goal.

00:10:55.875 --> 00:10:59.976
He wants to achieve everything on his own.

00:10:59.976 --> 00:11:04.625
Think about it - when Zoro gets lost from
the crew, this actually serves a crucial writing

00:11:04.625 --> 00:11:06.026
purpose for Oda.

00:11:06.026 --> 00:11:10.876
It’s not just a gag because often when the
crew gets divided into different groups, Zoro

00:11:10.876 --> 00:11:12.166
ends up on his own.

00:11:12.166 --> 00:11:18.136
In Sabaody, in Dressrosa, and Wano, just to
name a few, Zoro ends up in isolation from

00:11:18.136 --> 00:11:21.046
the rest of the crew going on a solo adventure.

00:11:21.046 --> 00:11:29.216
He perfectly captures the trope of the wandering
ronin.

00:11:29.216 --> 00:11:42.086
However, Zoro’s greatest character development
throughout the series has been overcoming

00:11:42.086 --> 00:11:44.646
this lone wolf persona.

00:11:44.646 --> 00:11:50.445
He’s grown close to his crew to the point
where he’s even willing to sacrifice his

00:11:50.445 --> 00:11:52.666
own dream for their sake.

00:11:52.666 --> 00:12:01.026
Let’s take a look at Zoro’s growth throughout
each arc and show how he has grown as a character.

00:12:01.026 --> 00:12:05.016
When Luffy first meets Zoro, his life is literally
on the line.

00:12:05.016 --> 00:12:10.516
His unwavering sense of honor has put him
in danger at the hands of someone as dishonorable

00:12:10.516 --> 00:12:11.586
as Helmeppo.

00:12:11.586 --> 00:12:16.116
He agrees to go for weeks without food or
water strung up on a cross, not knowing that

00:12:16.116 --> 00:12:22.086
the spoiled son of Axehand Morgan has no intention
of keeping his side of the bargain.

00:12:22.086 --> 00:12:27.216
He laughs off Luffy’s request to join him
and become a pirate - he is a pirate hunter

00:12:27.216 --> 00:12:32.656
after all - but after Luffy saves him from
execution he gains a newfound respect for

00:12:32.656 --> 00:12:34.326
the captain.

00:12:34.326 --> 00:12:38.806
Agreeing to join Luffy on his adventure seems
like just as good a path as any: after all,

00:12:38.806 --> 00:12:45.076
the two both have impossibly ambitious dreams
- to become the pirate king and the world’s

00:12:45.076 --> 00:12:49.236
greatest swordsman.

00:12:49.236 --> 00:12:53.995
This call to grand adventure parallels when
Rayleigh joined Roger - he’s swayed by the

00:12:53.995 --> 00:13:00.375
captain’s charisma and free spirit which
complements his own stoic and individual nature.

00:13:00.375 --> 00:13:05.125
Even though Zoro walked his path alone, if
he hadn’t joined up with his captain, he

00:13:05.125 --> 00:13:06.696
wouldn’t have survived.

00:13:06.696 --> 00:13:11.526
The next monumental step in Zoro’s character
growth is on the Baratie, when he goes face

00:13:11.526 --> 00:13:14.156
to face with Mihawk.

00:13:14.156 --> 00:13:21.726
He’s utterly defeated, but the warlord still
sees something important in Zoro.

00:13:21.726 --> 00:13:27.426
Even though he lacks power, he has the strength
of will to grow and become stronger.

00:13:27.426 --> 00:13:35.426
The exchange between the two swordsmen actually
foreshadows many of Zoro’s later feats.

00:13:35.426 --> 00:13:39.425
Mihawk defeats him with a tiny knife - much
like the knife Zoro uses at the beginning

00:13:39.425 --> 00:13:41.736
of Wano to escape his captors.

00:13:41.736 --> 00:13:48.206
He sees Mihawk slice a ship in half - a feat
Zoro is more than capable of after the timeskip.

00:13:48.206 --> 00:13:52.246
And Mihawk even refers to Zoro as “a frog
in a well” - an idiom with a similar meaning

00:13:52.246 --> 00:13:55.695
to the English “small fish in a big pond.”

00:13:55.695 --> 00:14:03.196
Later in Fishman Island, Zoro would say the
same to Hyouzou, the overly confident greatest

00:14:03.196 --> 00:14:05.166
swordsman of Fishman Island.

00:14:05.166 --> 00:14:09.196
In Arlong Park and Loguetown, Zoro comes to
respect Luffy more.

00:14:09.196 --> 00:14:13.636
When he confronts Arlong, he sees how far
Luffy is willing to go for someone he considers

00:14:13.636 --> 00:14:14.675
his nakama.

00:14:14.675 --> 00:14:19.685
In Loguetown, he sees Luffy’s fearlessness
in the face of death and how he’s willing

00:14:19.685 --> 00:14:25.245
to die if it means he’s working toward achieving
his dream.

00:14:25.245 --> 00:14:32.136
In Water 7, we really see Zoro step into the
role of the first mate.

00:14:32.136 --> 00:14:37.156
When Luffy and Usopp fight over the fate of
the Going Merry, the rest of the crew are

00:14:37.156 --> 00:14:38.156
devastated.

00:14:38.156 --> 00:14:40.495
Zoro, however, is the glue that keeps them
together.

00:14:40.495 --> 00:14:44.625
His coolheaded, stoic demeanor are crucial
in a situation like this.

00:14:44.625 --> 00:14:50.026
When everyone’s emotions are high, he occupies
the difficult position and does what must

00:14:50.026 --> 00:14:55.195
be done to maintain the sanctity of the Straw
Hats.

00:14:55.195 --> 00:15:01.945
He understands how difficult this conflict
is for Luffy, but even as Nami urges for compassion,

00:15:01.945 --> 00:15:05.706
Zoro understands that the crew must remain
loyal to Luffy.

00:15:05.706 --> 00:15:10.846
Zoro also refuses to allow Usopp to join while
deflecting with humor or antics.

00:15:10.846 --> 00:15:17.026
He’ll only allow Luffy to let him board
the ship if he issues a genuine apology for

00:15:17.026 --> 00:15:18.435
his misdeeds.

00:15:18.435 --> 00:15:23.175
Without Zoro’s strength and conviction,
the crew may have fractured permanently on

00:15:23.175 --> 00:15:24.175
Water 7.

00:15:24.175 --> 00:15:29.526
In the next arc, Thriller Bark, we get the
single most important Zoro moment in the entire

00:15:29.526 --> 00:15:30.616
series.

00:15:30.616 --> 00:15:32.796
“Nothing Happened.”

00:15:32.796 --> 00:15:37.396
When the entirety of the crew is incapacitated,
Kuma strikes a bargain with Zoro.

00:15:37.396 --> 00:15:42.615
The warlord will spare Luffy and the rest
of the crew, if Zoro agrees to take on all

00:15:42.615 --> 00:15:47.736
of the pain that Luffy suffered during the
battle - on top of Zoro’s own already severe

00:15:47.736 --> 00:15:50.505
strain from fighting Ryuma and Oars.

00:15:50.505 --> 00:15:55.865
This is the ultimate instance of Zoro shouldering
Luffy’s burdens and acting as a first mate.

00:15:55.865 --> 00:16:01.706
And moreover, despite being terribly wounded,
he stands with pride as he declares that “nothing

00:16:01.706 --> 00:16:02.706
happened.”

00:16:02.706 --> 00:16:04.226
Zoro doesn’t do the things he does for credit.

00:16:04.226 --> 00:16:09.806
He has complete satisfaction in knowing that
he’s done his duty.

00:16:09.806 --> 00:16:16.916
It’s just the same with becoming the world’s
greatest swordsman.

00:16:16.916 --> 00:16:19.796
He’s not doing it to gain clout or recognition
in the world.

00:16:19.796 --> 00:16:27.086
He simply wants to prove his strength to himself
and no one else.

00:16:27.086 --> 00:16:34.745
During the two year timeskip, Zoro demonstrated
just how loyal he really is to Luffy.

00:16:34.745 --> 00:16:39.656
In order to become stronger and help fight
for Luffy’s dream, he sacrificed his pride

00:16:39.656 --> 00:16:45.686
and begged for Dracule Mihawk to take him
as a student.

00:16:45.686 --> 00:16:48.876
Zoro began as a character who with a single
goal.

00:16:48.876 --> 00:16:50.506
He wanted to prove his own strength.

00:16:50.506 --> 00:16:57.026
But eventually, he found that through dedication
and loyalty, he could find an even greater

00:16:57.026 --> 00:16:58.026
purpose.

00:16:58.026 --> 00:17:04.746
By fighting for not only his goal, but for
Luffy’s dream as well, he unlocked true

00:17:04.746 --> 00:17:06.106
strength.

00:17:06.106 --> 00:17:10.085
Strength enough to become the world’s greatest
swordsman.

00:17:10.085 --> 00:17:11.825
CLIMAX

00:17:11.825 --> 00:17:15.796
This unbridled power, though, isn’t what
makes Zoro such a beloved character.

00:17:15.796 --> 00:17:19.796
It’s not just his bad ass fights - there
are plenty of characters who offer that kind

00:17:19.796 --> 00:17:20.796
of action.

00:17:20.796 --> 00:17:24.576
Zoro’s real draw is his honor.

00:17:24.576 --> 00:17:25.576
His compassion.

00:17:25.576 --> 00:17:26.576
His altruism.

00:17:26.576 --> 00:17:30.496
When Luffy found him, Zoro hadn’t been crucified
as a test of his own will.

00:17:30.496 --> 00:17:33.046
He was doing it to save an innocent little
girl.

00:17:33.046 --> 00:17:37.856
And even though Zoro walked a lonely road
in pursuit of his goal, he never truly walked

00:17:37.856 --> 00:17:38.856
alone.

00:17:38.856 --> 00:17:42.116
His dream was not just his - it was inherited.

00:17:42.116 --> 00:17:45.906
Zoro has never really fought only to prove
his strength to himself.

00:17:45.906 --> 00:17:49.456
He fights to prove his strength to Kuina.

00:17:49.456 --> 00:18:01.546
Through discipline, loyalty, honor, we don’t
just lift up ourselves, but lift up everyone

00:18:01.546 --> 00:18:02.576
around us.

00:18:02.576 --> 00:18:07.816
The beauty of Zoro’s character is how he
shows that real strength comes from unwavering

00:18:07.816 --> 00:18:09.376
dedication to others.

00:18:09.376 --> 00:18:10.376
TRANSITION

00:18:10.376 --> 00:18:13.236
And there is still so much more detail and
nuance with all of Zoro’s character.

00:18:13.236 --> 00:18:15.316
Especially for his character arc with Luffy.

00:18:15.316 --> 00:18:20.475
I have a whole video breaking it down in even
more detail right here.

